From: xbin <xbin@xbin.dev>
Subject: [PATCH] xbin single-tenant mode (-xbin-single-tenant)

Adds a mount mode for mounts that serve exactly one security domain (one
container-host tile's store, mounted into exactly one sandbox): ownership,
mode and special-file identity are virtualized into an encrypted xattr on
the cipher object (chown/chmod/mknod always succeed and round-trip), the
cipher tree stays uniformly daemon-owned 0700/0600, in-mount permission
checks are skipped (reaching the mountpoint IS the access decision), and
-allow_other is implied so sub-uid processes are not kernel-refused.
security.capability is served (encrypted) so file caps survive a store
round-trip; RENAME_WHITEOUT is decomposed into rename + virtual whiteout.

Because the daemon is the sole writer of a single-tenant cipher tree, the
identity plane is cached in memory per inode — including the
security.capability answer the kernel re-requests before every write
(issue #515) — so small-file workloads stay near stock speed.

Written against gocryptfs v2.6.1 for xbin's resenc container stores
(docs/resources.md); no on-disk format change — the identity xattr is an
overlay, trees mount fine with or without the flag.

Known fidelity gap: Linux forbids user.* xattrs on symlinks, so symlink
ownership is not virtualized (lchown on a symlink is a no-op).
